{"id":1922,"date":"2020-12-10T11:54:31","date_gmt":"2020-12-10T19:54:31","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1922"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:55:21","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:55:21","slug":"zumi-python-remote-control-and-conditionals","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-remote-control-and-conditionals\/","title":{"rendered":"1.2: Remote Control and Conditionals"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86c95\" name=\"6a22150f86c95\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Remote Control<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you understand drive commands, you can write your own remote control! First, import the Zumi library and create the object before you begin.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"prompt input_prompt\">\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from zumi.zumi import Zumi\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nzumi = Zumi()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86da3\" name=\"6a22150f86da3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>User input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]To make a remote control, you need a way to register your input and translate it to motor commands. Python includes a function\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">input()<\/span>\u00a0that reads the keyboard input and saves it as a variable. To see how this works, run the following code, type in your name, and hit enter. Any input that you entered will be saved in\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">my_name<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">my_name = input(\"What is your name? \")\r\nprint(\"Hello,\",my_name)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86e05\" name=\"6a22150f86e05\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Pseudocode<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you have all the tools you need, put it together in pseudocode, which is a program that\u2019s written in plain language that a human can understand. Pseudocode is a good way to figure out how your program will work before translating it to Python. One small problem:\u00a0<span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">input()<\/span>\u00a0can\u2019t read arrow key controls, so your remote control code will be checking for letter inputs &#8220;w&#8221;, &#8220;a&#8221;, &#8220;s&#8221;, and &#8220;d&#8221;. Look at the pseudocode below to see what we mean.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-family: Courier;\">get input from user<br \/>\nif input is &#8220;w&#8221;, go forward<br \/>\nif input is &#8220;s&#8221;, reverse<br \/>\nif input is &#8220;a&#8221;, turn left<br \/>\nif input is &#8220;d&#8221;, turn right<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1930&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86e94\" name=\"6a22150f86e94\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>If statements<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>If my alarm goes off, then I will wake up. Else, I will stay asleep.<\/li>\n<li>If I am hungry, I will eat something.<\/li>\n<li>If it&#8217;s sunny out, I will wear sunscreen.<\/li>\n<li>If my room is clean, I will get my allowance. Else, I will make no money.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>If statements are written like this in Python:<\/p>\n<p>if insert condition here:<br \/>\nInsert statements here<br \/>\nelse:<br \/>\nInsert statements here<\/p>\n<p>if alarm goes off:<br \/>\nI will wake up<br \/>\nelse:<br \/>\nStay asleep[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1931&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The condition is what the computer checks to know if it is true or false.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the condition is true, the computer will execute, or carry out, the statements underneath it.<\/li>\n<li>If the condition is false, the computer will NOT execute the statements underneath it.<\/li>\n<li>&#8220;Else\u201d has no condition! This is because \u201celse\u201d is the last option when using if-else statements. When a computer reaches the \u201celse\u201d option after checking all the other conditions, then the else condition is automatically true and will run its statements.<\/li>\n<li>One way to use \u201celse\u201d would be when something needs to happen when a condition is false. When the &#8220;if&#8221; condition is false and nothing needs to happen, don\u2019t use an \u201celse\u201d.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86eee\" name=\"6a22150f86eee\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Indentation<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Indentation is very important in the Python programming language. The indentations indicate which statements belong to a particular section in the code.<\/p>\n<p>You may have noticed that the statements below the if and else statements are indented 4 spaces or one tab space. Even if the indent is off by\u00a0<em>one<\/em>\u00a0space, you will get an error! Try to stick to either tabs or spaces, and don&#8217;t combine the two.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86f97\" name=\"6a22150f86f97\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Comparators<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing text_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"text_cell_render border-box-sizing rendered_html\">\n<p>Next, translate your pseudocode to Python code. Before inputting drive commands, use print statements for testing, like this one:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">direction = input(\"Please enter a command: \")\r\n\r\nif direction == \"w\":\r\nprint(\"Go forward\")<\/pre>\n<p>The equal sign may look familiar, but in code it has a slightly different application! The sign is an example of a\u00a0<strong>comparator<\/strong>. Comparators can be used inside conditions to compare values. If the statement is true, the statement below it will execute. These are comparators:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>&gt; Greater Than<\/li>\n<li>\\&lt; Less Than<\/li>\n<li>&gt;= Greater Than or Equal To<\/li>\n<li>&lt;= Less Than or Equal To<\/li>\n<li>== Equal To<\/li>\n<li>!= Not Equal To<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Run the cell below and make some changes to the conditionals to see how these work! If you want, you can change the variable names or the input statements. Make sure to test your code.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"cell border-box-sizing code_cell rendered\">\n<div class=\"input\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">X= 10\r\nY = 100\r\nZ = 200\r\n\r\nif X != Y:\r\n    print(\"X is not equal to Y\")\r\n\r\nif Z &gt; Y:\r\n    print(\"Z is greater than Y\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now you have all the tools to write out the rest of the missing conditionals. Use if statements, comparators, and print statements to write code for the &#8220;s&#8221;, &#8220;a&#8221;, and &#8220;d&#8221; keys.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#TODO Finish the rest of the missing if statements\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n    direction = input(\"Please enter a command: \")\r\n\r\n    if direction == \"w\":\r\n        print(\"Go forward\")<\/pre>\n<p>Did you notice the code is running forever? Because this is a remote control code, you want to be able to continuously tell Zumi what to do. To do this, you need to use a while loop that runs forever. Currently, the only way to stop the code is by clicking the &#8220;stop&#8221; icon in the toolbar. To safely quit from this loop, it is better to include an if statement that checks for &#8220;q&#8221; input to break from the loop.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">if direction ==\"q\":\r\n    break<\/pre>\n<p>Add this code to your code above and test that you can exit your code without errors.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f86fe3\" name=\"6a22150f86fe3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Add drive commands<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that you have the logic down, add the drive commands with the correct command. Remember you can change the parameters for these controls or choose different keys to customize your remote control![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22150f87034\" name=\"6a22150f87034\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Extension Activities<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h3><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-1932 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2020\/12\/engineering_extension-1-150x150.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"115\" height=\"115\" \/>Lombard Street<\/h3>\n<p>Lombard Street is one of the most crooked streets in San Francisco. What would Zumi need to do to be able to drive down it? Construct your own model of Lombard Street with whatever materials you choose to use. Walls should be used around the street, and Lombard Street itself is at a 27% slope. Next, measure the turn degree of each hairpin turn to emulate on the model. After streets are finished, test it out with your remote code, noting how many degrees you had to turn their Zumi in order to navigate it safely.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Remote Control [vc_column_text]Now that you understand drive commands, you can write your own remote control! First, import the Zumi library and create the object before you begin.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-remote-control-and-conditionals\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2050,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,2119,2123,2121,2122,2134,2111,2230],"class_list":["post-1922","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-conditionals","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-remote-control","lesson-tag-zumi","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1922","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/w